  "text": "A simple laboratory method for creating a three‑layer semiconductor device with alternating conductivity types is presented. The approach involves melting a low‑melting‑point metal into the opposite surfaces of a single‑crystal wafer that originally conducts electrons. This straightforward alloying step consistently yields devices that show useful electrical behavior. Results from a typical batch of 118 similarly prepared units are summarized, showing the spread of performance values. The devices achieved voltage amplification up to about 46 dB, a current transfer ratio (alpha) as high as 0.997, and a noise figure (expressed as 1 kc) as low as 6 dB. The data demonstrate that the method can reliably produce high‑gain, low‑noise transistors suitable for experimental work."
